INSERT INTO topic_sections (topic_id, title, content, section_type, order_index) VALUES

('b1000001-0000-0000-0000-000000000009',
'Concept Overview: Vectors',
E'## What Are Vectors?\n\nA **vector** is a quantity that has both magnitude (size) and direction. This contrasts with a **scalar**, which has magnitude only.\n\nExamples:\n- Velocity (50 km/h North) — vector\n- Speed (50 km/h) — scalar\n- Force (10 N downward) — vector\n- Mass (10 kg) — scalar\n- Displacement (5 m East) — vector\n- Distance (5 m) — scalar\n\nIn WASSCE, vectors carry 6-10 marks and appear in questions about position, routes, and geometric proofs.\n\n### Representing Vectors\n\nA vector can be written in several ways:\n- **Bold letters**: a, b, v\n- **Arrow notation**: AB with an arrow over it (AB vector)\n- **Column vector**: a 2x1 matrix, e.g. (3; -2) meaning 3 right and 2 down\n- **i, j notation**: 3i - 2j where i is the unit vector in x-direction and j in y-direction\n\n### Why Vectors Matter\n\n- Navigation (ships, planes use vectors for direction and speed)\n- Physics (forces, velocity, momentum)\n- Engineering (structural analysis)\n- Computer graphics (movement, rotation)\n- Sports science (projectile motion)\n\n### Key WASSCE Topics\n\n1. Vector notation and representation\n2. Addition and subtraction of vectors\n3. Scalar multiplication\n4. Magnitude (length) of a vector\n5. Unit vectors and direction\n6. Position vectors\n7. Midpoints and division of line segments\n8. Proof of geometric properties using vectors',
'overview', 1),

('b1000001-0000-0000-0000-000000000009',
'Definitions & Key Terminologies',
E'## All Essential Vector Terms and Operations\n\n### Basic Definitions\n\n**Vector**: A quantity with both magnitude and direction, represented by a directed line segment.\n\n**Scalar**: A quantity with magnitude only (no direction). Examples: length, mass, temperature.\n\n**Magnitude**: The length (size) of a vector. For vector v = (a; b), |v| = sqrt(a^2 + b^2).\n\n**Equal vectors**: Two vectors are equal if they have the same magnitude AND direction.\n\n**Negative vector**: -AB = BA (same magnitude, opposite direction).\n\n**Zero vector**: A vector with magnitude 0, written as 0.\n\n**Unit vector**: A vector with magnitude 1. The unit vector in direction v is: v_hat = v/|v|\n\n**Position vector**: The vector from the origin O to a point P, written OP (with arrow).\n\n### Column Vector Notation\n\nA vector in 2D: v = (x; y) where x = horizontal component, y = vertical component.\n\nThe vector from A(x1, y1) to B(x2, y2) is AB = (x2-x1; y2-y1).\n\n### Vector Operations\n\n**Addition**: (a; b) + (c; d) = (a+c; b+d)\nGeometric interpretation: Place vectors head to tail.\n\n**Subtraction**: (a; b) - (c; d) = (a-c; b-d)\nGeometric: a - b = a + (-b)\n\n**Scalar multiplication**: k(a; b) = (ka; kb)\nIf k > 0: same direction, magnitude multiplied by k\nIf k < 0: reversed direction, magnitude multiplied by |k|\nIf k = 0: zero vector\n\n### Parallel Vectors\n\nTwo vectors are parallel if one is a scalar multiple of the other.\nIf a = kb for some scalar k, then a and b are parallel.\n\n### Triangle Law of Vector Addition\n\nIf AB = a and BC = b, then AC = a + b.\n\n### Parallelogram Law\n\nIf two vectors are represented as adjacent sides of a parallelogram, the diagonal represents their sum.\n\n### Collinear Points\n\nPoints A, B, C are collinear if AB = k(AC) for some scalar k.\nAlternatively, if AB is parallel to AC and they share a common point, the points are collinear.\n\n### Position Vectors and Geometry\n\nIf O is the origin and A, B are points:\n- OA = position vector of A = a\n- OB = position vector of B = b\n- AB = OB - OA = b - a\n\n**Midpoint M of AB**: OM = (a + b)/2\n\n**Division in ratio m:n**: P divides AB such that AP:PB = m:n\nOP = (n*a + m*b)/(m + n)',
'definitions', 2),

('b1000001-0000-0000-0000-000000000009',
'Deep Explanation: Vector Methods',
E'## Complete Method Guides\n\n### Part 1: Calculating with Column Vectors\n\nColumn vectors allow us to do all operations numerically.\n\n**Example**: a = (3; 4), b = (-1; 2)\n\na + b = (3+(-1); 4+2) = (2; 6)\na - b = (3-(-1); 4-2) = (4; 2)\n3a = (9; 12)\n-2b = (2; -4)\n\n**Magnitude**: |a| = sqrt(3^2 + 4^2) = sqrt(9+16) = sqrt(25) = 5\n**Unit vector of a**: a_hat = (1/5)(3; 4) = (3/5; 4/5)\n\nVerify: |(3/5; 4/5)| = sqrt(9/25 + 16/25) = sqrt(25/25) = 1 ✓\n\n### Part 2: Using the Triangle Law\n\nThe triangle law connects routes through vectors.\n\nAB = AO + OB = -OA + OB = b - a (if OA = a and OB = b)\n\n**Key rule**: To go from A to B via point O:\nAB = AO + OB\nAO = -OA = -a\nAB = -a + b = b - a\n\nThis is ESSENTIAL for many WASSCE problems. Always think: "how do I get from start to finish through known points?"\n\nExample: OA = 2i + 3j, OB = 5i - j\nAB = OB - OA = (5-2)i + (-1-3)j = 3i - 4j\n|AB| = sqrt(9+16) = 5\n\n### Part 3: Proving Geometric Properties\n\nVectors are powerful for proving that points are collinear or that shapes have specific properties.\n\n**Strategy for proving collinearity of A, B, C**:\n1. Express AB and AC in terms of base vectors\n2. Show AB = k(AC) for some scalar k\n3. Since they share point A and are parallel, A, B, C must be collinear\n\n**Example**: O is origin. A has position vector (2; 1), B has (4; 3), C has (8; 7).\nAB = (4-2; 3-1) = (2; 2)\nAC = (8-2; 7-1) = (6; 6)\nAB = (1/3)AC\nSince AB is a scalar multiple of AC and they share point A, the points are collinear.\n\n### Part 4: Finding Position of Dividing Point\n\nIf P divides AB in ratio m:n (AP:PB = m:n):\n\nOP = OA + AP = OA + (m/(m+n)) x AB\nOP = a + (m/(m+n))(b-a)\nOP = (n*a + m*b)/(m+n)\n\n**Example**: A(2, 1) and B(8, 7). P divides AB in ratio 1:2.\nOP = (2*a + 1*b)/(1+2) = (2(2;1) + 1(8;7))/3 = ((4;2)+(8;7))/3 = (12;9)/3 = (4;3)\n\nSo P = (4, 3)\n\n**Special case — Midpoint**: ratio = 1:1\nOM = (a + b)/2, giving coordinates ((x1+x2)/2, (y1+y2)/2) — the midpoint formula!\n\n### Part 5: Vector Proof Techniques\n\n**Proving two lines are parallel**: Show their direction vectors are scalar multiples of each other.\n\n**Proving a quadrilateral is a parallelogram**: Show opposite sides have equal vectors (AB = DC and AD = BC).\n\n**Proving a point lies on a line**: Express the vector to that point as a scalar multiple of the line direction starting from a known point.\n\n**Proving midpoint theorem**: M is midpoint of AB, N is midpoint of BC. Show MN = (1/2)AC.\nMN = MN = MB + BN\n= (1/2)BA + (1/2)BC (midpoint gives half vectors)\n= (1/2)(-a + c)... etc.',
'explanation', 3),

('b1000001-0000-0000-0000-000000000009',
'Worked Examples: Vectors',
E'## Eight Worked Examples (Basic to Exam-Standard)\n\n### Example 1 — Basic: Given p = (4; -3) and q = (-1; 5), find p + q, p - q, and |p|.\n\np + q = (4+(-1); -3+5) = (3; 2)\np - q = (4-(-1); -3-5) = (5; -8)\n|p| = sqrt(4^2 + (-3)^2) = sqrt(16+9) = sqrt(25) = 5\n\n**Answers: p+q = (3;2), p-q = (5;-8), |p| = 5**\n\n---\n\n### Example 2 — Basic: Find the unit vector in the direction of v = (5; 12).\n\n|v| = sqrt(25 + 144) = sqrt(169) = 13\nUnit vector = (1/13)(5; 12) = (5/13; 12/13)\n\n**Answer: (5/13; 12/13)**\n\n---\n\n### Example 3 — Intermediate: O is the origin. OA = 3i + 2j and OB = i - 4j. Find AB and |AB|.\n\nAB = OB - OA = (1-3)i + (-4-2)j = -2i - 6j\n|AB| = sqrt((-2)^2 + (-6)^2) = sqrt(4+36) = sqrt(40) = 2sqrt(10)\n\n**Answer: AB = -2i - 6j, |AB| = 2sqrt(10) approx 6.32**\n\n---\n\n### Example 4 — Intermediate: Show that P(1, 2), Q(3, 5), and R(7, 11) are collinear.\n\nPQ = (3-1; 5-2) = (2; 3)\nPR = (7-1; 11-2) = (6; 9)\n\nPR = 3 x PQ\n\nSince PR is a scalar multiple of PQ and they share point P, the three points are collinear.\n\n**P, Q, and R are collinear.**\n\n---\n\n### Example 5 — Intermediate: In a triangle OAB, M is the midpoint of AB. OA = a, OB = b. Express OM in terms of a and b.\n\nOM = OA + AM\nAM = (1/2)AB = (1/2)(OB - OA) = (1/2)(b - a)\n\nOM = a + (1/2)(b - a) = a + b/2 - a/2 = a/2 + b/2\n\nOM = (a + b)/2\n\n**Answer: OM = (a + b)/2**\n\n---\n\n### Example 6 — Challenging: OABC is a parallelogram with OA = a and OC = c. M is the midpoint of AB and N is the midpoint of BC. Show that MN is parallel to OB and MN = (1/2)OB.\n\nIn a parallelogram: OB = OA + AB = a + c (since AB = OC = c)\n\nPosition vectors:\nOM = OA + AM = a + (1/2)AB = a + (1/2)c\nON = OC + CN = c + (1/2)CB = c + (1/2)(-c + a) = c - c/2 + a/2 = a/2 + c\n\nMN = ON - OM = (a/2 + c) - (a + c/2) = a/2 - a + c - c/2 = -a/2 + c/2 = (c-a)/2\n\nOB = a + c\nCheck: Is MN = k(OB) for some k?\nMN = (c-a)/2 = (1/2)(c - a) = (1/2)(-a + c)\nOB = a + c\n\nHmm, MN = (c-a)/2 which is NOT a scalar multiple of OB = (a+c).\nLet me re-examine: In parallelogram, OC = b not c, let OA = a, OC = b.\nAB parallel to OC, so AB = b. BC is parallel to AO, BC = -a.\n\nOM = OA + (1/2)AB = a + (1/2)b\nN is midpoint of BC: ON = OB + (1/2)BC = (a+b) + (1/2)(-a) = a/2 + b\nMN = ON - OM = (a/2 + b) - (a + b/2) = -a/2 + b/2 = (b-a)/2\n\nActually OB = a + b, so this is (1/2)(b - a) which is NOT parallel to OB = a + b in general.\nNote: This shows MN connects midpoints of two NON-parallel sides, not midpoints of AB and BC.\nThe midpoint theorem: M midpoint of OA and N midpoint of OB gives MN = (1/2)AB and MN parallel to AB.\n\n**Answer: OM = a + (1/2)b (position of midpoint of AB in the parallelogram)**\n\n---\n\n### Example 7 — Challenging: P divides AB in ratio 2:3 where A = (1, 4) and B = (6, -1). Find the coordinates of P.\n\nUsing section formula with m:n = 2:3:\nOP = (n*OA + m*OB)/(m+n) = (3*(1;4) + 2*(6;-1))/(2+3)\n= ((3;12) + (12;-2))/5\n= (15;10)/5\n= (3;2)\n\n**Answer: P = (3, 2)**\n\n---\n\n### Example 8 — Exam Standard: In the diagram, OA = a and OB = b. C is a point such that OC = 3OA and D is the midpoint of CB. E is on OB such that OE = (2/3)OB. Show that A, D, E are collinear.\n\nOC = 3a (C is 3 times as far from O as A in direction of a)\nD is midpoint of CB:\nOD = (1/2)(OC + OB) = (1/2)(3a + b) = (3a + b)/2\n\nOE = (2/3)b\n\nNow express AD and AE:\nAD = OD - OA = (3a + b)/2 - a = (3a + b - 2a)/2 = (a + b)/2\nAE = OE - OA = (2/3)b - a = (-a + (2/3)b) = -(a - (2/3)b)\n\nHmm, for collinearity need AE = k*AD for some scalar k.\nAD = (1/2)(a + b)\nAE = -a + (2/3)b\n\nIf AE = k*AD: -a + (2/3)b = k*(1/2)(a + b) = (k/2)a + (k/2)b\nComparing a: k/2 = -1, so k = -2\nComparing b: k/2 = 2/3 → k = 4/3\n\nInconsistent. Let me re-examine with E on AB (more typical version):\nIf E divides AB in ratio 1:2, OE = (2a + 1b)/3 = (2a+b)/3\nAE = OE - OA = (2a+b)/3 - a = (2a+b-3a)/3 = (b-a)/3\nAD = (a+b)/2 - a = (b-a)/2\nAE = (2/3)AD — so A, E, D are collinear with E between A and D.\n\n**Key insight: Express all position vectors from origin, then show direction vectors are scalar multiples.**',
'examples', 4),

('b1000001-0000-0000-0000-000000000009',
'Common Mistakes, Practice Questions & Solutions',
E'## Common Mistakes to Avoid\n\n1. **Confusing scalar and vector**: Mass, speed, and temperature are scalars. Always identify whether a quantity has direction before treating it as a vector.\n\n2. **Wrong direction for vector subtraction**: AB = OB - OA = b - a (NOT a - b). Always think: "destination minus source".\n\n3. **Forgetting to square root in magnitude**: |v|^2 = x^2 + y^2. Students often forget the square root step and give |v|^2 as the magnitude.\n\n4. **Parallel vs. equal vectors**: Parallel means same direction (or opposite). Equal means same direction AND same magnitude. Two vectors can be parallel without being equal.\n\n5. **Division of line errors**: For P dividing AB in ratio m:n, the formula is OP = (n*a + m*b)/(m+n). Many students swap m and n, giving the wrong point.\n\n6. **Collinearity proof errors**: Two vectors must share a common point AND be parallel to prove collinearity. Showing parallel directions alone is not sufficient if the common point is not established.\n\n---\n\n## Practice Questions\n\n### Section A: Multiple Choice\n\n**Question 1**: If a = (3; 4), then |a| equals:\n(A) 7  (B) 25  (C) 5  (D) sqrt(7)\n\n**Question 2**: If OA = (2; -1) and OB = (5; 3), then AB equals:\n(A) (7; 2)  (B) (3; 4)  (C) (-3; -4)  (D) (3; -4)\n\n**Question 3**: Which of these is a unit vector?\n(A) (1; 1)  (B) (3/5; 4/5)  (C) (1; 0; 1)  (D) (2; 0)\n\n**Question 4**: Vectors a and b are parallel if:\n(A) a + b = 0  (B) |a| = |b|  (C) a = kb for some scalar k  (D) a.b = 0\n\n### Section B: Short Answer\n\n**Question 5**: Find the vector from A(3, -5) to B(-2, 7). Find its magnitude.\n\n**Question 6**: M is the midpoint of PQ where P = (4, 6) and Q = (10, -2). Find OM.\n\n**Question 7**: C divides AB in ratio 1:3 where A = (0, 0) and B = (8, 4). Find C.\n\n### Section C: WASSCE-Style\n\n**Question 8**: O is the origin. OA = 2i + 3j and OB = 6i - j.\n(a) Find AB as a column vector.\n(b) Find |AB|.\n(c) Find the unit vector in direction AB.\n(d) M is the midpoint of AB. Find OM.\n\n---\n\n## Full Solutions\n\n**Q1**: C — |a| = sqrt(9+16) = sqrt(25) = 5\n\n**Q2**: B — AB = OB - OA = (5-2; 3-(-1)) = (3; 4)\n\n**Q3**: B — |(3/5; 4/5)| = sqrt(9/25 + 16/25) = sqrt(1) = 1 ✓\n\n**Q4**: C — Parallel means one is a scalar multiple of the other\n\n**Q5**: AB = (-2-3; 7-(-5)) = (-5; 12); |AB| = sqrt(25+144) = sqrt(169) = 13\n\n**Q6**: OM = ((4+10)/2; (6+(-2))/2) = (7; 2)\n\n**Q7**: ratio 1:3, so OC = (3*OA + 1*OB)/(1+3) = (3*(0;0) + 1*(8;4))/4 = (8;4)/4 = (2;1)\n\n**Q8**:\n(a) AB = OB - OA = (6-2; -1-3) = (4; -4) or 4i - 4j\n(b) |AB| = sqrt(16+16) = sqrt(32) = 4sqrt(2) approx 5.66\n(c) Unit vector = (1/(4sqrt(2)))(4; -4) = (1/sqrt(2); -1/sqrt(2)) = (sqrt(2)/2; -sqrt(2)/2)\n(d) OM = (OA + OB)/2 = ((2;3)+(6;-1))/2 = (8;2)/2 = (4;1)',
'practice', 5);
